 ### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-- **Run-to-Run Variance:** ModelNet fine-tuning exhibits appreciable run-to-run variation, particularly on ModelNet40. Results should be interpreted with caution, and provenance must be tracked [S1, unofficial].
-- **Protocol Matching:** When comparing methods, ensure the Gaussian count and attribute sets match exactly (e.g., 1K Gaussians for both pretraining and transfer) to avoid confounding factors [S1, unofficial].
-- **Camera Bias:** EG3D focuses on frontal views, while PanoHead supports 360°. Training configurations must align with the target view distribution to avoid artifacts in unseen angles [S2].
+- **Run-to-run Variance:** ModelNet fine-tuning exhibits appreciable run-to-run variation, particularly on ModelNet40 [S2, unofficial]. Results should be interpreted with caution regarding small cross-environment differences [S2, unofficial].
+- **Protocol Matching:** Ensure that pre-training and transfer protocols match (e.g., 1K-to-4K protocol) to avoid unfair comparisons [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
-### Error-Driven 2D Gaussian Training
-Focuses on 2D Gaussian primitives with error-driven densification, differing in primitive dimensionality and densification triggers. See [Error-Driven 2D Gaussian Training](13985-error-driven-2d-gaussian-training.md).
+### Standard Downstream Protocols
+A standardized training and evaluation pipeline for classification and part segmentation tasks using Gaussian inputs [S2, unofficial]. It differs from generative decoder training by using fixed Gaussian counts (1K/2K) and epoch-based training rather than iteration-based training. It is chosen when evaluating the geometric fidelity of Gaussians for downstream 3D vision tasks [S2, unofficial].
 
-### DashGaussian
-Optimizes for specific rendering efficiency and compression, differing in attribute quantization and rendering pipeline. See [DashGaussian](15742-dashgaussian.md).
+### StopThePop Framework Integration
+A framework that integrates occlusion-aware densification with StopThePop's per-pixel depth ordering to remove popping artifacts and keep occlusion boundaries intact [S3, unofficial]. It differs from standard training by modifying the densification statistics accumulation to be occlusion-aware [S3, unofficial]. It is chosen for large-scale scenes with heavy occlusion where standard view-count normalizers diverge [S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Training time is dominated by the number of iterations (500k for decoders) and batch size. Classification tasks are shorter (300 epochs) but require careful handling of variance [S1, unofficial][S2].
-- **Memory Complexity:** Depends on the number of Gaussians (1K or 2K) and attributes. Grouping (32 Gaussians/group) may facilitate memory-efficient updates but does not change the total memory footprint significantly [S1, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Standardized protocols allow for fair comparison across different Gaussian-based methods. Fixed grouping simplifies implementation for downstream tasks.
-- **Limitations:** High variance in ModelNet results can obscure method improvements. Frontal-view bias in EG3D limits applicability to full 3D space rendering compared to PanoHead [S1, unofficial][S2].
-- **When to Use:** Use for fine-tuning pre-trained Gaussian encoders for classification/segmentation or for training Gaussian decoders for GANs. Not suitable for general novel-view synthesis without adaptation.
+- **Time Complexity:** Training for 500,000 iterations is computationally expensive [S1]. Downstream protocols use fewer epochs (300) but may require careful tuning due to variance [S2, unofficial].
+- **Memory Complexity:** Depends on the number of Gaussians (1K-2K for downstream, variable for generative) [S1][S2, unofficial].
+- **Strengths:** High-quality 3D reconstruction for generative models [S1]; standardized evaluation for 3D classification [S2, unofficial].
+- **Limitations:** High variance in ModelNet results [S2, unofficial]; requires careful camera sampling strategy for generative models [S1].
 
 ## Background
-The training configurations described originate from studies on Gaussian-JEPA and Gaussian-MAE for 3D Gaussian splats, as well as Gaussian Splatting Decoders for 3D-aware GANs. These works established standardized protocols for evaluating Gaussian representations on downstream tasks, addressing the need for reproducible benchmarks in the rapidly evolving field of Gaussian-based rendering [S1, unofficial][S2]. The distinction between frontal (EG3D) and 360° (PanoHead) camera sampling highlights the adaptation of Gaussian splatting to different generative modeling constraints [S2].
+Originated from the application of 3D Gaussian Splatting to 3D-aware generative adversarial networks (GANs) [S1] and subsequent adaptations for 3D point cloud classification and segmentation [S2, unofficial]. The StopThePop integration addresses occlusion issues in large-scale scenes [S3, unofficial].
 
